WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:14.986
Narrator: Many years ago, England was going through hard times.

2
00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:17.947
Narrator: Some villages were very poor.

3
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:23.210
Narrator: But everyone still needed to eat and laugh.

4
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.711
Narrator: This is a story about how strangers become friends.

5
00:00:29.684 --> 00:00:32.607
Morris: I say, Stanley, this looks like a good spot.

6
00:00:33.988 --> 00:00:34.848
Stanley: Right-o, Morris.

7
00:00:35.189 --> 00:00:36.670
We'll stop here for the evening.

8
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.572
Morris: Shall we start making the soup?

9
00:00:40.493 --> 00:00:41.514
Stanley: A fine idea.

10
00:00:41.954 --> 00:00:43.836
I'll get the water and a stone.

11
00:00:43.856 --> 00:00:48.860
Narrator: Morris and Stanley were on their way to London.

12
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.802
Narrator: They were traveling on foot.

13
00:00:50.822 --> 00:00:55.946
Narrator: Along the way Morris and Stanley stopped at villages.

14
00:00:55.966 --> 00:00:59.801
Narrator: Each time, they made stone soup.

15
00:01:00.722 --> 00:01:02.984
Stanley: Morris, old pal, here's the water.

16
00:01:03.004 --> 00:01:06.407
Morris: Smells delicious.

17
00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:08.849
Stanley: And here's a stone.

18
00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:11.791
Morris: That's a fine one, Stanley.

19
00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.234
Our soup will be very tasty tonight.

20
00:01:15.254 --> 00:01:18.136
Narrator: Just then Mrs.

21
00:01:18.276 --> 00:01:23.140
Tweedy from the village [n3@and her son Wilbert walked by.

22
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.202
Narrator: They were returning from the market.

23
00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:27.764
Morris: Good day, madam.

24
00:01:29.128 --> 00:01:30.449
Mrs. Tweedy: Good day, gentlemen.

25
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:33.672
Stanley: Would you care to join us for some soup?

26
00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:36.254
Wilbert: Oh, yes please!

27
00:01:36.274 --> 00:01:38.276
Mrs. Tweedy: Wilbert!

28
00:01:38.736 --> 00:01:41.598
We must get home and prepare supper.

29
00:01:41.618 --> 00:01:43.280
Morris: Wilbert?

30
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:44.461
Is that your name?

31
00:01:44.481 --> 00:01:46.823
Wilbert: Yes, sir, it is.

32
00:01:46.843 --> 00:01:52.267
Morris: Why, that boy with the three-legged cat was named Wilbert too.

33
00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:55.249
Wilbert: Three-legged cat?

34
00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.292
What happened to its other leg?

35
00:01:59.084 --> 00:02:06.589
Morris: Well, it was born with four legs, of course, but then Narrator: Morris and Stanley told the

36
00:02:06.629 --> 00:02:09.091
story of the [n3@boy with the three-legged cat.

37
00:02:09.111 --> 00:02:12.393
Narrator: Even Wilbert's mother listened.

38
00:02:13.494 --> 00:02:15.055
Narrator: Just then Mr.

39
00:02:15.095 --> 00:02:17.136
Podger from the village walked by.

40
00:02:17.156 --> 00:02:20.438
Narrator: He was carrying a loaf of bread.

41
00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:22.580
Morris: And that's the story.

42
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.801
Wilbert: Is it true?

43
00:02:24.821 --> 00:02:27.083
Mr. Podger: Is what true?

44
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:28.664
What's going on here?

45
00:02:30.028 --> 00:02:30.668
Mrs. Tweedy: Oh, Mr.

46
00:02:30.728 --> 00:02:34.149
Podger, please come and meet Morris and Stanley.

47
00:02:34.169 --> 00:02:37.670
Narrator: Everyone said hello to each other.

48
00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.452
Stanley: Morris, is it just me or does Mr.

49
00:02:41.572 --> 00:02:43.312
Podger remind you of someone?

50
00:02:43.332 --> 00:02:46.113
Morris: Hmm, yes.

51
00:02:46.513 --> 00:02:49.774
He reminds me of Otis and Oliver, the twin barbers!

52
00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:52.715
Stanley: Exactly what I was thinking.

53
00:02:52.735 --> 00:02:54.816
Mr. Podger: Twin barbers?

54
00:02:55.396 --> 00:02:57.572
Where? Stanley: In Windermere.

55
00:02:57.592 --> 00:02:59.653
They're quite the barbers, [n3@Otis and Oliver.

56
00:02:59.693 --> 00:03:01.974
You see Narrator: Mr.

57
00:03:02.034 --> 00:03:06.416
Podger sat down and listened to the story of the twin barbers.

58
00:03:06.436 --> 00:03:11.158
Narrator: A few minutes later, Miss Mabel from the village walked by.

59
00:03:11.178 --> 00:03:14.500
Narrator: She was carrying a bag of potatoes.

60
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.761
Miss Mabel: Good day, all.

61
00:03:17.401 --> 00:03:19.202
May I ask what you're doing here?

62
00:03:19.222 --> 00:03:21.283
Mr. Podger: Miss Mabel!

63
00:03:21.943 --> 00:03:23.544
Come and meet Morris and Stanley.

64
00:03:24.832 --> 00:03:27.614
Morris: I say, Miss Mabel, what a splendid coat.

65
00:03:27.634 --> 00:03:29.596
Miss Mabel: Why, thank you.

66
00:03:29.616 --> 00:03:34.220
Stanley: It looks like the one worn by Queen Francesca of Spain.

67
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.842
Morris: Indeed!

68
00:03:37.282 --> 00:03:38.563
We met her in Chester.

69
00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:41.746
Miss Mabel: A coat like mine on a queen?

70
00:03:42.446 --> 00:03:43.007
Do tell all!

71
00:03:43.027 --> 00:03:49.532
Narrator: So Morris and Stanley told the story of the Spanish queen.

72
00:03:49.552 --> 00:03:51.534
Narrator: Everyone laughed.

73
00:03:52.494 --> 00:03:55.708
They had a great time [n3@talking with the strangers.

74
00:03:55.728 --> 00:03:58.989
Narrator: Before long, it was getting dark.

75
00:03:59.009 --> 00:04:02.810
Mrs. Tweedy: Thank you for a lovely time.

76
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.791
Mr. Podger: I haven't laughed so much in ages.

77
00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:10.192
Miss Mabel: Oh, I hope your soup is not ruined.

78
00:04:10.212 --> 00:04:13.913
Wilbert: Why is there a stone in the soup?

79
00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:16.854
Morris: Because it's stone soup.

80
00:04:16.874 --> 00:04:20.435
Mrs. Tweedy: That doesn't sound very tasty.

81
00:04:20.455 --> 00:04:23.356
Stanley: It's actually quite good.

82
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.522
Morris: Last night it tasted of turnips.

83
00:04:26.542 --> 00:04:29.664
Stanley: And the night before, it tasted of peas.

84
00:04:29.684 --> 00:04:35.488
Narrator: Now, the villagers did not think stone soup could be tasty.

85
00:04:35.528 --> 00:04:38.970
Narrator: They looked at each other and wondered.

86
00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.952
Morris: Oh yes, this is very tasty.

87
00:04:42.853 --> 00:04:44.054
What do you think, Stanley?

88
00:04:44.074 --> 00:04:45.655
Stanley: Delicious!

89
00:04:46.195 --> 00:04:49.037
But I think it needs just a little extra something.

90
00:04:49.057 --> 00:04:52.920
Mrs. Tweedy: I have some carrots and onions here.

91
00:04:53.608 --> 00:04:55.409
Mrs. Tweedy: Let's put them in the soup.

92
00:04:55.429 --> 00:04:58.051
Miss Mabel: I have some potatoes.

93
00:04:58.672 --> 00:04:59.232
In they go!

94
00:04:59.252 --> 00:05:02.374
Mr. Podger: And I've got fresh bread.

95
00:05:02.694 --> 00:05:04.376
Just the thing to have with soup.

96
00:05:04.396 --> 00:05:09.179
Narrator: Stanley and Morris took turns stirring the pot.

97
00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:15.383
Narrator: Soon the stone soup turned into a hearty vegetable soup.

98
00:05:15.564 --> 00:05:18.246
Stanley: The best I've ever tasted!

99
00:05:18.266 --> 00:05:21.408
Help yourselves!

100
00:05:23.264 --> 00:05:24.445
Morris: Delicious!

101
00:05:24.765 --> 00:05:31.688
Which reminds me of another story Narrator: So Morris told them another funny story.

102
00:05:31.708 --> 00:05:34.229
Narrator: Everyone ate and laughed.

103
00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.130
It was a wonderful evening.

104
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.452
Mr. Podger: You must come and stay the night at my house.

105
00:05:40.472 --> 00:05:42.433
Morris and Stanley: Oh, thank you, sir!

106
00:05:42.453 --> 00:05:49.136
Narrator: Morris and Stanley left the next morning.

107
00:05:50.184 --> 00:05:53.407
Narrator: But the villagers never forgot the two men.

108
00:05:53.427 --> 00:05:59.414
Narrator: They often talked about how Morris and Stanley made them laugh.

109
00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:05.740
Narrator: And everyone remembered their wonderfully delicious stone soup.

110
00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.604
Narrator: The people of this town had been strangers.

111
00:06:09.624 --> 00:06:12.728
Narrator: But now they were friends.
